{"name":"Yawl","hex":"#547497","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yawl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small ship's boat, usually rowed by four or six oars.","A fore-and-aft rigged sailing vessel with two masts, main and mizzen, the mizzen stepped abaft the rudder post."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cry out; to howl."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yawl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yawl","blocks":[{"text":"A yawl is a type of boat. The term has several meanings. It can apply to the rig, to the hull type or to the use which the vessel is put."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Chubasco_photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/330px-Chubasco_photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yawl","wikidata":"Q1137441"},{"title":"YAWL","blocks":[{"text":"YAWL is a workflow language based on workflow patterns. It is supported by a software system that includes an execution engine, a graphical editor and a worklist handler. It is available as open-source software under the LGPL license."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YAWL","wikidata":"Q2164484"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png/500px-26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png/1280px-26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png/500px-26_ft_Yawl_RMG_J0921.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The lines plan of a Royal Navy 26 ft (7.9 m) yawl, dated 1799. The transom stern differentiates this type from the double-ended, clinker-built working craft.","link":"https://en.